The class sits in the **root package** so component scanning covers every feature package you add beneath it.

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class InventoryApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var ctx = SpringApplication.run(InventoryApplication.class, args);
        // run() has returned -> app is fully bootstrapped and serving
        System.out.println("Beans loaded: " + ctx.getBeanDefinitionCount());
    }
}

@Service
class InventoryService {
    InventoryService() { System.out.println("1) constructor: bean instantiated"); }

    @PostConstruct
    void init() { System.out.println("2) @PostConstruct: dependencies wired"); }
}

@Component
class StartupRunner implements CommandLineRunner {
    @Override
    public void run(String... args) {
        System.out.println("3) CommandLineRunner: runs after server starts");
    }
}
```

The printed order — constructor, then `@PostConstruct`, then `CommandLineRunner` — mirrors the lifecycle phases exactly.

